# --- OSINT Portal environment ---
# Copy to .env.local for local overrides.

# Comma-separated allow-list of targets segments may run against
# (owned / authorized resources only). CIDRs, IPs, IPv6 and domains accepted.
# Defaults (without this variable): loopback, private (10/8, 172.16/12,
# 192.168/16) plus the `localhost` hostname. Link-local (169.254/16, fe80::/10)
# is NOT allowed by default — on a cloud host that is the instance metadata
# service, so allowing it would make the portal a credential-exfiltration relay.
# Raw IP/CIDR targets are always gated; read-only DNS/registry segments
# (dig, host, whois) additionally accept public hostname targets.
# OSINT_ALLOWED_TARGETS=10.0.0.0/8,example.com,127.0.0.1

# Hard timeout (ms) applied per tool run.
# (sherlock defaults to 60s when unset; others to 20s)
# OSINT_RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=20000

# Max captured stdout/stderr bytes per run.
# OSINT_MAX_OUTPUT_BYTES=64000

# --- Local runner (tool execution) ---
# The site can be deployed to the internet while the OSINT binaries stay on
# your machine: the browser talks to this runner over loopback, because a
# serverless function has no route to 127.0.0.1 on your PC. Start it with
# `npm run runner`.

# Where the browser should find the runner. This is the URL other visitors'
# own machines will be asked about, so it must be a loopback address.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_RUNNER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787

# Port the runner listens on (loopback only — it is never exposed publicly).
# RUNNER_PORT=8787

# Extra browser origins allowed to drive the runner, comma-separated.
# Always allowed without configuration: http://localhost:*, http://127.0.0.1:*
# and https://*.vercel.app. Add your own domain if you serve the site elsewhere.
# RUNNER_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://osint.example.com

# Where the runner keeps the case file (evidence + graph survive restarts).
# OSINT_CASE_FILE=./.osint-portal/evidence.json

# --- Anya (AI assistant) ---
# On a public deployment leave these empty: visitors bring their own provider
# key in the chat UI, it is kept in their browser and never reaches this server.
# Set them only when you run the site yourself and want your own key used.
# Provider chain (first that answers wins):
#   1. Ollama (local, no key)   — used automatically when reachable
#   2. Groq                     — needs ANYA_GROQ_API_KEY (free tier)
#   3. OpenRouter               — needs ANYA_OPENROUTER_API_KEY (free :free models)
#   4. Generic OpenAI-compatible endpoint — ANYA_API_KEY + ANYA_BASE_URL + ANYA_MODEL
# With nothing configured Anya stays online in "offline brain" mode.
# ANYA_OLLAMA_URL=http://127.0.0.1:11434
# ANYA_MODEL=qwen2.5:3b
# ANYA_GROQ_API_KEY=
# ANYA_OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# ANYA_API_KEY=
# ANYA_BASE_URL=
# ANYA_TIMEOUT_MS=30000